var GEOGRAPHY_TOPICS=[
{id:"physical-geography",t:"Physical geography",min:8,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Physical geography studies Earth's natural features and processes, including landforms, water, climate, soils, and ecosystems. It asks how these systems form, where they occur, and how they change over time.",details:[["Earth's connected systems","Mountains influence winds and rainfall; rivers carry water and sediment from highlands toward coasts; climate shapes soils and vegetation. Physical geographers connect processes across the atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere, and solid Earth instead of treating each landscape feature in isolation."],["How it is studied","Field observations, maps, satellite imagery, sensors, and computer models help geographers compare places and track change. A study might map a river's course, compare vegetation across a rainfall gradient, or examine how a volcanic island formed. Scale matters: a process visible across a continent can look different at a neighborhood scale."]] },
{id:"human-geography",t:"Human geography",min:8,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Human geography examines where people live and how societies shape, use, and experience places. It studies population, migration, culture, cities, economies, and the relationships between communities and their surroundings.",details:[["People and place","Settlement patterns reflect many influences, including jobs, transport, history, resources, policy, and environmental conditions. Human geographers ask why activities cluster in some places, how people move between regions, and how identity and culture shape landscapes."],["Patterns and evidence","Researchers use censuses, interviews, maps, historical records, and observation to understand social patterns. They compare data across locations and time while considering who is represented and whose experiences may be missing. A map can reveal a pattern, but further evidence is needed to explain its causes."]] },
{id:"environmental-geography",t:"Environmental geography",min:8,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Environmental geography connects physical and human geography. It studies how people affect environments and how environmental conditions and changes influence communities.",details:[["Human–environment relationships","Land use, energy production, transport, and consumption can change habitats, water quality, and climate. Environmental conditions also affect where people can live and work, how resources are managed, and which communities face particular hazards. These relationships vary by place and are shaped by social and economic choices."],["Assessing change","Environmental geographers combine field measurements, satellite observations, public records, and community knowledge. They may map deforestation, compare pollution exposure, or assess vulnerability to flooding. Good analysis distinguishes observed change from its possible causes and notes the uncertainty in available data."]] },
{id:"cartography-gis",t:"Cartography and geographic information systems",min:8,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Cartography is the design and interpretation of maps. Geographic information systems (GIS) store, combine, analyze, and display information tied to locations on Earth.",details:[["Maps are models","Every map selects and simplifies information for a purpose. Projection changes how a round Earth is shown on a flat surface, while scale determines how much detail fits. Symbols, labels, colors, and boundaries guide interpretation, so map design can make some patterns easier to see than others."],["What GIS can do","GIS layers can combine features such as roads, elevation, land cover, and population. Spatial analysis can measure distance, identify overlap, or compare conditions across regions. Results depend on data quality, coordinate systems, dates, and the assumptions used to define each layer."]] },
{id:"climatology",t:"Climatology and climate geography",min:8,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Climatology studies long-term patterns in weather, while climate geography examines how those patterns vary across places and affect landscapes and people. Climate describes typical conditions and variability over decades, not the weather on a single day.",details:[["Climate patterns","Latitude, elevation, distance from oceans, winds, and ocean currents influence temperature and precipitation. Climate regions are useful summaries, but conditions can vary within a region because mountains, coastlines, and local land cover shape the environment."],["Evidence and impacts","Climate scientists analyze long records from weather stations, ocean measurements, satellites, and other sources. Geographers connect these patterns with agriculture, water supply, ecosystems, and settlement. Maps of climate risk can guide planning, but local exposure and resources affect how communities experience hazards."]] },
{id:"geomorphology",t:"Geomorphology and landforms",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Geomorphology explains how landforms develop and change. It studies processes such as weathering, erosion, landslides, river transport, glaciation, and volcanic activity.",details:[["Shaping the landscape","Rock type, tectonic forces, water, ice, wind, and gravity interact to create features such as valleys, cliffs, dunes, and deltas. A landform records processes acting over different timescales, from a sudden landslide to gradual river erosion over thousands of years."],["Reading the evidence","Geomorphologists survey terrain, examine sediments and rocks, use elevation data, and monitor active processes. Comparing landforms helps reconstruct how a landscape formed and assess how it may respond to storms, development, or changing climate. Similar shapes can form through different processes, so multiple clues are useful."]] },
{id:"hydrology",t:"Hydrology and water geography",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Hydrology studies water as it moves through the atmosphere, across land, into rivers and lakes, and underground. Water geography also examines how people access, use, manage, and share water.",details:[["The water cycle","Sunlight drives evaporation, plants release water vapor, and cooling air can produce clouds and precipitation. Some precipitation runs over land into streams, while some infiltrates soil and replenishes groundwater. The balance changes with terrain, vegetation, soil, climate, and human infrastructure."],["Measuring water","Hydrologists monitor stream flow, rainfall, groundwater levels, and water quality. Maps of watersheds show where water drains, helping communities plan for floods and supplies. Measurements vary over time, and a river basin may cross administrative borders, making coordination important."]] },
{id:"biogeography",t:"Biogeography and the distribution of life",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Biogeography studies where plants, animals, and ecosystems occur and why their distributions vary. It links geography and biology by considering climate, history, barriers, and interactions among species.",details:[["Why species live where they do","Temperature, water, soils, food, competition, and habitat influence whether a species can persist in a place. Mountains, oceans, and deserts can limit movement, while past climate shifts and continental changes help explain present-day patterns."],["Mapping biodiversity","Researchers combine field surveys, museum collections, satellite observations, and species records to map distributions. Such maps help identify regions with many unique species and track range shifts. Records are unevenly distributed, so a blank area on a map does not necessarily mean a species is absent."]] },
{id:"population-geography",t:"Population geography",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Population geography studies where people live, how populations change, and how those patterns relate to place. It examines settlement density, age structure, migration, births, and deaths.",details:[["Population patterns","People are distributed unevenly because of history, environment, employment, transport, and policy. Population density summarizes how many people live in an area, but it can hide important differences between crowded neighborhoods and sparsely settled regions."],["Understanding change","Censuses, surveys, birth and death records, and migration data help researchers track population change. These measures have limits: people may be missed, categories may differ, and data can become outdated. Geographers compare multiple sources to understand trends and their local context."]] },
{id:"urban-geography",t:"Urban geography and cities",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Urban geography studies cities and towns: how they grow, how land is used, how people and goods move, and how urban life differs across neighborhoods.",details:[["City structure and change","Homes, workplaces, services, roads, parks, and industrial areas form patterns shaped by history, markets, transport, and planning. Cities expand and change as populations, economies, and infrastructure shift. These processes can create opportunities as well as unequal access to housing and services."],["Mapping urban life","Urban geographers analyze building footprints, transit networks, land use, population, and local experience. Maps can show travel access or green space, but averages may hide differences among residents. Combining spatial data with community input gives a fuller picture of how a city functions."]] },
{id:"economic-geography",t:"Economic geography",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Economic geography examines where economic activities happen and how places are connected through work, trade, transport, resources, and production. It considers why industries cluster in some regions and how economic change affects communities.",details:[["Networks and location","Businesses choose locations based on factors such as workers, customers, materials, transport, energy, and policy. Supply chains connect producers and consumers across many places. A local change—such as a new port or factory—can affect employment and land use far beyond its immediate site."],["Regional differences","Economic geographers compare jobs, income, industries, and access to services across areas. Statistics can reveal broad patterns, but do not by themselves explain people's lived experiences or the full causes of inequality. Historical context and local conditions help interpret the numbers."]] },
{id:"cultural-geography",t:"Cultural geography",min:7,xp:25,sk:"sci",lv:"Cadet",body:"Cultural geography explores how culture, identity, language, belief, food, art, and shared memory shape places and landscapes. It also studies how ideas and practices move, mix, and change across regions.",details:[["Culture in landscapes","Buildings, street names, farms, monuments, and public spaces can reflect a community's history and values. Cultural landscapes are not fixed: people reinterpret places, preserve some traditions, and introduce new practices over time."],["Studying meaning and movement","Cultural geographers use interviews, archives, observation, maps, and media to learn how people understand places. They examine migration and cultural exchange while avoiding the assumption that every member of a group shares the same experience. Place meanings can differ and sometimes be contested."]] }
];
